\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces High-level architecture. Solid arrows show forward dispatch; the dashed arrow shows the return path used by voice-agent webhooks and Android SMS-reply ingestion.}}{11}{figure.caption.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Sequence diagram for an AI voice call. Solid arrows are forward dispatch; dashed arrows are returns (audio streams and the terminal callback). The defensive 5-minute poll job (not drawn) is cancelled when the callback arrives first.}}{15}{figure.caption.7}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Contact rate by channel and attempt. WhatsApp dominates at every attempt, while the ``any channel'' bars confirm that multi-channel orchestration buys an additional 4--8 percentage points over the best single channel at each attempt.}}{24}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Cumulative recovery curve over a thirty-day cycle for an equivalent cohort of Bucket~0 cases, under the agency-only baseline (blue) and the platform (red). The platform recovers 70~percent of allocations within the first five days and reaches an 85~percent cycle-end rate against the baseline's 38~percent and 64~percent respectively; the gap is largest in the first five days, which is where the multi-channel orchestration and after-hours AI calling contribute most.}}{26}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces AI voice agent turn-end-to-agent-start latency at the p50, p95 and p99 percentiles. The shaded band marks the 800--900~ms human-perception threshold; the p50 sits below it, the p95 already crosses it and the p99 is roughly $2.5\times $ the upper edge of the band.}}{27}{figure.caption.15}%
